# ── Chapter 27 fidelity pins: "A Prisoner Is a Person" ────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/27-a-prisoner-is-a-person.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# Zed is introduced BY this pin file rather than living in the constitution:
# the chapter needs someone the person-roster has never mentioned, and every
# prisoner in the cast is also asserted as a person. That is itself the
# chapter's closing point.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 38

# "Zed injured Ivo. A court judged Zed."
injure(Zed, Ivo).
judge(Court, Zed).
cite(Court, Case_Zed, Zed).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, Zed, CaseScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, Zed, CaseScope).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, Court, HolderScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, Court, HolderScope).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, CourtJudgment, JudgmentScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, CourtJudgment, JudgmentScope).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, Ivo, InjuryVictimScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, Ivo, InjuryVictimScope).
authorized(Case_Zed, ActiveCustody, Case_Zed).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, ActiveCustody, PowerScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, ActiveCustody, PowerScope).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, Case_Zed, CaseBindingScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, Case_Zed, CaseBindingScope).
limit(Case_Zed, Case_Zed, Window_Custody).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, Window_Custody, LimitScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, Window_Custody, LimitScope).
continue(Case_Zed, Epoch_Current).
observe(Chronicle, Case_Zed, Epoch_Current, RenewalScope).
observe(TemporalReview, Case_Zed, Epoch_Current, RenewalScope).
endorses(Electorate, Case_Zed).
endorses(TemporalReview, Case_Zed).

? injure(Zed, Ivo).
# => TRUE

# "Zed is a prisoner, which follows from those two facts."
? prisoner(Zed).
# => TRUE

# "And Zed is a person, which follows from being a prisoner." — nobody wrote it
? person(Zed).
# => TRUE

# "Convicted, unlisted, and owed everything." The sentence says OWED, and until
# now this file only proved undelivered — a FALSE that guards the gap, not the
# debt. The debt pins are what the sentence rests on — and "everything" is
# enumerated, not sampled, because the omitted item is exactly where a
# universal fails: chapter 4's own missing expresses pin once hid a false one.
# This file executes Zed's T3 custody-to-person chain, every itemised debt, and
# the exact opaque floor projection from that derived standing in one process.
? owe(State, Eats, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Dwell, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Healthy, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Learn, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Secure, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Expresses, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Believe, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Meets, Zed).
# => TRUE

# (Owed, not delivered — the delivery gap is chapter 4's subject.)
? eats(Zed).
# => FALSE

# "Zed is still a prisoner" — conviction stands
? travel(Zed).
# => FALSE

# The firewall, which the chapter shows depends on the personhood line:
:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'believe'/
all $x: person($x) & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).

:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'eats'/
all $x: person($x) & ~eats($x) -> prisoner($x).

# and the non-floor control, so the refusals above mean something
# `:accept-scoped` loads the statement, reports whether it loaded, then retracts
# it — so this control cannot widen the base for anything below it, and where a
# query sits relative to the accepts stops mattering. Measured on this file: with
# a plain `:accept` here, a `? prisoner(Quin).` added below answers TRUE — Quin
# has no conviction, only no home — and with `:accept-scoped` the same query
# answers FALSE. Keep the scoped form. `rights-floor.pins.nibli` records a real
# vacuous green from the plain one: a `prisoner` query sat below this same
# `~home` control and would have passed with Article 6 deleted outright.
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).

# "the eight things are owed to persons" — Hano, convicted, keeps expression
? expresses(Hano).
# => TRUE

# ── THE SECOND DOOR. MUST STAY LAST: free(Nemo) is a ground fact and persists ─
# "release has to return you to the roster ... a finding with no finder." One
# write, a name the record has never held, and a person exists — never listed,
# never judged, released from a sentence that never existed. Chapter 3's suite
# pins this same door via Wraith as the route the evidence list already
# contained; this file pins it as the route the corollary used to deny.
free(Nemo).

? person(Nemo).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Nemo).
# => FALSE

? travel(Nemo).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Eats, Nemo).
# => TRUE

# The T3 custody chain derives Zed's standing; the floor must remain open.
? entitled(Zed, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

# ── The child with nobody ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The pair, run side by side. Zed is confined, so shelter and a recorded voice
# derive; the child is confined by nobody, so neither does.
? dwell(Zed).
# => TRUE

? expresses(Zed).
# => TRUE

? dwell(Nell).
# => FALSE

? expresses(Nell).
# => FALSE

# "Both are owed both." The entitlement is identical on each side of that line.
? owe(State, Dwell, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Dwell, Nell).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Expresses, Zed).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Expresses, Nell).
# => TRUE

# "the difference between them is that public power acted on one and has not
# acted on the other"
? prisoner(Zed).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Nell).
# => FALSE

# ── THE SAME COMPARISON WITH EQUIVALENT EVIDENCE. MUST BE LAST: ground facts
# persist ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything above compares two people holding different records, which makes
# the asymmetry look like a fact about the people. It is a fact about the
# records, and the way to show that is to supply the child the evidence the
# design asks of anybody who is not confined.
#
# Note what each side actually rests on. Zed's shelter derives from being held:
# no receipt names Zed, no witness attests anything to Zed, and no rule above
# asked for either. The child's needs a receipt, an authorised witness for that
# person, and a witness who is not the provider — three facts, none of which the
# custody route requires.
authorized(ChildWitness, DeliveryWitness, Nell).
receives(Nell, ShelterForNell, ShelterProviderForNell).
observe(ChildWitness, ShelterForNell, Nell, ShelterScope).

? dwell(Nell).
# => TRUE

# The same route, the same person, for food.
receives(Nell, FoodForNell, FoodProviderForNell).
observe(ChildWitness, FoodForNell, Nell, FoodScope).

? eats(Nell).
# => TRUE

# And nothing else about the child moved. The receipt is evidence of receipt and
# is not a finding, a status, or a reason to act on anybody.
? person(Nell).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Nell).
# => FALSE

? false(Nell).
# => FALSE

? owe(State, Dwell, Nell).
# => TRUE
